Name the DLL after its .def file

src/DllMain.def declares LIBRARY CascLib.dll, so the DLL's export directory
and the import library GNU ld generates from it both name CascLib.dll. CMake,
however, calls a MinGW shared library lib<OUTPUT_NAME>.dll, and the file is
installed as libCascLib.dll. Every consumer then imports a DLL that does not
exist and cannot start -- launched from an MSYS2 shell it exits with status
127.

Drop the prefix from the runtime DLL, which is what the comment above the
property already intends ("On Win32, build CascLib.dll"). The import library
keeps its lib prefix (libCascLib.dll.a), and MSVC builds, whose prefix is
empty anyway, are unaffected.

Upstream: not submitted yet
Applies-to: all environments

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -128,7 +128,7 @@
     )
     # On Win32, build CascLib.dll
     if(WIN32)
-        set_target_properties(casc PROPERTIES OUTPUT_NAME CascLib)
+        set_target_properties(casc PROPERTIES OUTPUT_NAME CascLib PREFIX "")
     endif()
     target_compile_definitions(casc PUBLIC ${PUBLIC_COMPILE_DEFINITIONS})
 endif()
